WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.690
The second episode of the final season of
Game of Thrones was a tense, character driven

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:09.780
exploration of the moments building up to
the arrival of the Night King's army of the

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.480
dead and the impending Battle of Winterfell.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.380
Between a love scene that took viewers by
complete surprise, a sorrowful song sung around

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:22.550
the fire, a knighting that should have happened
ages ago, and Jon Snow dropping the bombshell

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.279
of all bombshells, fans are still buzzing.

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:30.279
But the thing that has people really talking
is the plan to protect the castle's most vulnerable

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:33.210
residents by hiding them in the crypt below
the castle.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.149
"I'm going to be in the crypt with my son.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.320
And I'd feel a lot better with you down there
to protect us."

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.360
"Alright.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.440
I'll defend the crypt then."

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.300
On first blush, it seems to make perfect sense.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.700
There's no area more secure in Winterfell
than the crypts, after all.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.960
There are only a couple entry points, and
any invading army would need to fight their

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.409
way through the castle's formidable defenses
- and defenders - to reach it.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:59.789
There's one major problem, though: this isn't
just any army.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:01.239
It's the army of the dead.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:06.240
Led by a guy whose source of power is the
ability to raise the dead and control them.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.320
And guess what the crypt is filled with?

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.880
Uh-oh.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.880
Naturally, fans are freaking out about this
seemingly obvious flaw in Jon's plan.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.140
One fan wrote on Twitter, "Everyone's going
to hide in the crypt with all the dead Starks

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:23.860
while an army who can raise the dead approaches
and no one forsees a problem with that?"

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:24.860
And another tweeted,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.790
"They keep mentioning how safe the crypt is
going to be.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:31.630
After years of watching this show, I'm sure
that means the opposite."

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.969
Of course, this was hinted at in the promo
clips for the final season, as the first teaser

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:40.270
was called "The Crypts of Winterfell," and
it showed the icy grip of death coming for

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.969
our characters in the crypts.

00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:46.860
And the episode three tease also hints at
this with clips such as Arya Stark fleeing

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.870
an unseen foe inside the castle, not to mention
this foreboding line from Daenerys:

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.799
"The dead are already here."

00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:58.329
Perhaps most chilling is the fact that there
could be some familiar faces among the Night

00:01:58.329 --> 00:01:59.860
King's new minions.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.570
After all, this is the Stark family crypt,
meaning Jon's mom Lyanna is among those who

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.210
could rise, as is Rickon, who died in the
Battle of the Bastards.

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.370
And could we even see a headless Ned zombie?

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.940
The mind shudders at the thought.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.470
"Your husband was an honorable man.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.220
He should rest beside his family in the crypts
beneath Winterfell."

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.330
Though this horrifying theory may be a surprise
to fans of the show, it's no surprise to fans

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:26.360
of the books.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.300
In fact, this scene was seemingly foreshadowed
way back in the very first of George R.R.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.110
Martin's “A Song of Ice and Fire” novels,
where Jon Snow has a recurring nightmare about

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:37.980
this exact thing happening:

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.010
"Last night he had dreamt the Winterfell dream
again.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.810
He was wandering the empty castle, searching
for his father, descending into the crypts…

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:49.730
When he turned he saw that the vaults were
opening, one after the other.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:54.580
As the dead kings came stumbling from their
cold black graves..."

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.560
Of course, this being Game of Thrones, there's
really no way of knowing what will happen,

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.340
or who will live or die.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:05.550
If luck is on the side of the living, perhaps
Arya will use her specially made dragonglass-outfitted

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:10.590
spear to slay wights and White Walkers galore,
and the little girl who swore to defend the

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.830
crypt will keep her promise.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.590
With fan favorites like Tyrion set to be in
the crypts, though, fans are worried that

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.660
their worst fears will come to pass - and
we may even see some of our most beloved characters

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.760
then rise from the dead to fight for the Night
King.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:29.230
All we know for sure is that not everyone
will survive the next episode of Game of Thrones

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.560
- and if this show gets any more tense, we
might not survive watching it ether.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.880
"The things we do for love."

